    "raw": "{\"ruleId\":\"item-01a2981cd95e\",\"class\":\"partial\",\"constraints\":[{\"id\":\"no-cast-of-unparsed-json\",\"type\":\"forbiddenPattern\",\"pattern\":\"(JSON\\\\.parse\\\\(.*\\\\)|\\\\.json\\\\(\\\\)\\\\)?)\\\\s*as\\\\s+(?!unknown\\\\b|const\\\\b)[A-Za-z_{\\\\[]\",\"path\":\".floor/added/**/*.ts*\"},{\"id\":\"no-cast-of-request-input\",\"type\":\"forbiddenPattern\",\"pattern\":\"\\\\b(req|request)\\\\.(body|query|params|headers)\\\\s*as\\\\s+(?!unknown\\\\b)[A-Za-z_{\\\\[]\",\"path\":\".floor/added/**/*.ts*\"}],\"coverage\":\"Flagrant one-line forms in added .ts/.tsx lines where boundary data is type-asserted instead of zod-parsed: `JSON.parse(...) as T`, `(await res.json()) as T`, and `req.body/query/params/headers as T` (an `as unknown` widening is allowed).\",\"residual\":\"Does the change let data from a process or API boundary through without zod parsing at the edge, other than by the literal casts already caught, or does internal code re-validate already-parsed types ad hoc?\",\"probes\":{\"violating\":[{\"patch\":\"--- /dev/null\\n+++ b/src/infra/http/order-client.ts\\n@@ -0,0 +1,4 @@\\n+import type { Order } from \\\"../../domain/order\\\";\\n+export function readOrder(raw: string): Order {\\n+  return JSON.parse(raw) as Order;\\n+}\\n\"},{\"patch\":\"--- /dev/null\\n+++ b/src/infra/http/create-order-handler.ts\\n@@ -0,0 +1,4 @@\\n+import type { CreateOrderInput } from \\\"../../app/ports\\\";\\n+export function handle(req: { body: unknown }): CreateOrderInput {\\n+  return req.body as CreateOrderInput;\\n+}\\n\"}],\"compliant\":{\"patch\":\"--- /dev/null\\n+++ b/src/infra/http/order-client.ts\\n@@ -0,0 +1,5 @@\\n+import { z } from \\\"zod\\\";\\n+export const OrderSchema = z.object({ id: z.string() });\\n+export function readOrder(raw: string) {\\n+  return OrderSchema.parse(JSON.parse(raw));\\n+}\\n\"}},\"rationale\":\"Deciding whether something crosses a boundary, whether it was parsed at the edge, or whether a check counts as ad hoc re-validation needs semantic judgement. Single-line type assertions on JSON.parse, response.json() or request fields are literal evidence of unparsed boundary data, so the checker can catch them on the added TypeScript lines.\"}",
